:: S9-End-Theodora-Marriage
The Prince pulls Theodora toward him. 'You are a woman of unexpected depths,' he says softly. She blinks at him, then says, 'I hope you are not angry.' He laughs, and touches her cheek; and she kisses him. 'Well,' says the old lady briskly, standing. 'How many unexpected turns life brings to us.' Theodora and the Prince are married.

:: S9-End-Lucinda-Marriage
The Prince gives a great sigh and puts his head down on Lucinda's lap. 'I was sure I wouldn't find you,' he says. 'It seemed impossible.' 'You have me now,' she says, and puts her hand on the back of his neck, while Theodora slips out of the room, and the old lady rings for brandy. Lucinda and the Prince are married.

:: S9-End-Peace
There is the crack of a summoning: and there she stands, in the doorway, the secret daughter, the enchantress. 'May I present my third daughter,' says the old lady, very dryly. The Prince frowns at Cinderella a little longer, then seems to reach some decision. No one is punished, no shoe is forced, and the evening ends in peace, with brandy all around.

:: S9-End-Disaster
Cinderella puts the shoe on, turning her ankle to watch the effect. The change sweeps up through her, making her taller, more perfect, almost inhumanly beautiful. The Prince drops his hand. 'It's true,' says Lucinda. 'We poured holy water on her every day for a year.' 'But the law,' says the Prince. It all ends in disaster, as evenings with magic so often do.

:: S9-End-Prince-Departs
'Check the rest of your maidens,' the old lady says. 'Good luck finding your bride. You will not discover her in this house.' 'Thank Providence for that,' he says, snatching up the glass slipper. You watch him go, and think of the third daughter, waiting, in her bedroom, with the door locked. The Prince departs in anger.

:: S9-End-Cinderella-Executed
'You need not have done that,' the Prince says. 'You could have broken it. Lied to me. Remained hidden.' 'She understands,' says the old lady, rising from her chair. 'I'm afraid the rest of us, not being in love with you, had less faith in your intuition.' The Prince swallows. 'You see I cannot conceal this; it would be treason, now I have seen this witchcraft with my own eyes.' Cinderella is executed.

:: S9-End-Cinderella-Wed
'This business of the shoe is nonsense,' the Prince remarks. He turns and hurls the slipper against the wall, where it splinters into dozens of sparkling pieces. There is a great sigh in the room, and the old lady actually laughs. His gaze lingers on Cinderella. She looks back at him, her chin tilted up. Cinderella and the Prince are (eventually) wed.

:: S9-End-Pirates
You speak very loudly. 'If that happens again,' says the old woman to you, 'we will sell you to the first man with an earring we can find.' Lucinda's cheeks go pink. 'Right,' says the old lady. And she rings for a servant to remove you from the room. You are sold to pirates and have a glorious career on the open sea.

